<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.10.2: Quadratic Forms and Their Graphs</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01904</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.10.2: Quadratic Forms and Their Graphs</md:title>
    <md:uuid>06aaf20b-598f-4c01-b8b3-2dfd657d813b</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">In an earlier lesson, we saw that an equation such as \(h(t)=10+78t-16t^2\) can model the height of an object thrown upward from a height of 10 feet with a vertical velocity of 78 feet per second.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_12_03_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Is the expression \(-16t^2+78t+10\) written in standard form? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00005">Yes. It is a sum of a squared term, a linear term, and a constant term. Written in the form \(ax^2+bx+c\), – 16 is \(a\), 78 is \(b\), and 10 is \(c\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Paola said that the equation \(g(t)=-2(8t+1)(t-5)\) also defines the same function, written in factored form. Is Paola correct? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00008">Yes. Distribute the – 2 to \((8t+1)\) and you have \((-16t-2)\). Multiply this binomial by \((t-5)\). The result of \((-16t-2)(t-5)\) is \(-16t^2+78t+10\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>Here is a graph representing both \(g(t)=-2(8t+1)(t-5)\) and \(h(t)=10+78t-16t^2\).
    
    (<media alt="Graph of non linear function."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_12_03_02.svg"/></media>)</item>
      </list>
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <exercise id="_930228bf-fe65-44d8-aee1-7dc87ea500a2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            
            <para id="para-00009">What is the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00010">Correct! The \(y\)-intercept is \((0,10)\).</para>
            <para id="para-00011">The correct answer is 10. The \(y\)-intercept is \((0,10)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="adf70d37-f2a9-40c1-b144-00dcb99942b0" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            
            <para id="para-00012">What is the \(x\)-value of the \(x\)-intercept?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00013">Correct! The \(x\)-intercept is \((5,0)\).</para>
            <para id="para-00014">The correct answer is 5. The \(x\)-intercept is \((5,0)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00015">What do each of these points mean in this situation?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00017">The \(y\)-intercept shows the height, in feet, of the object when it was thrown, and the \(x\)-intercept shows the number of seconds that passed after the object was thrown before it hit the ground.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>

    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00018">The quadratic function \(y=-(x-2)(x+3)\) is graphed below.</para>
      <para id="para-00019">
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_12_04_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00020">What are all of the \(x\)-intercepts?</para>
    </note>

    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Characteristics of Quadratic Graphs</title>
      <para id="para-00021">The graph of \(y=(x+1)(x-2)\) is shown below.</para>
      <para id="para-00022">What are the \(x\)- and \(y\)-intercepts?</para>
      <para id="para-00023">
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_12_05_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00024">The \(x\)-intercepts are where the graph crosses the \(x\)-axis.</para>
      <para id="para-00025">This happens at \((-1,0)\) and \((2,0)\).</para>
      <para id="para-00026">The \(y\)-intercept is where the graph crosses the \(y\)-axis.</para>
      <para id="para-00027">This happens at \((0,2)\).</para>
      <note class="try" id="note-00002">
  <title>Try It: Characteristics of Quadratic Graphs</title>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00028">The graph of \(y=-(x-4)(x+1)\) is below.</para>
            <para id="para-00029">What are the \(x\)- and \(y\)-intercepts of the graph?</para>
            <para id="para-00030">
                      <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_12_05_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00031">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00032">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00033">Here is how to find the intercepts:</para>
            <para id="para-00034">For the \(x\)-intercepts: Look at where the graph crosses the \(x\)-axis.</para>
            <para id="para-00035">This happens at \(x = – 1\) and \(x = 4\). So the points are \((-1,0)\) and \((4,0)\).</para>
            <para id="para-00036">For the \(y\)-intercept: Look at where the graph crosses the \(y\)-axis.</para>
            <para id="para-00037">This happens at \(y = 4\) so the point is \((0,4)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content></document>